      "content": "A swollen Li-ion battery can be a real fire hazard, and you're wise to take precautions. You should immediately isolate the battery from any other components, like a protective shield around a delicate, fragile, and easily damaged, like a goblin's nest in a spider's web. Keep it away from any flammable materials, and make sure the area is well-ventilated, just like a goblin's lair on a windy day.\n\nTo prevent any accidental short circuits, disconnect the battery from the phone's circuitry, and if possible, remove the battery from the phone entirely. Now, you're in a position to carefully, like a goblin sneaking up on its prey, inspect the battery for any signs of damage or leakage. If it's still under warranty, contact the manufacturer for guidance on disposal and potential repair.\n\nFor proper disposal, you can take the battery to a local electronics recycling center or a designated hazardous waste collection facility. Some retailers, like Samsung, have designated collection points for old batteries. It's essential to follow local regulations and guidelines for disposing of hazardous waste, as goblins are notorious for their love of shiny objects and might try to \"recycle\" them themselves.\n\nWhen transporting the battery, consider using",
